 so the client uploads directly to R2, and the server never sees the file.\\n\\n However, when R2 is not configured, the code falls back to local storage. In that case, the direct upload route writes the file to the server\\'s disk (in the public/uploads directory). This is what the user wants to avoid.\\n\\n The user\\'s idea: if we set an environment variable (LOCAL_STORE) to true, then we store the file in the system\\'s temporary directory (on the server) and then immediately remove it? But that doesn\\'t make sense because we need the file for processing (like transcoding, thumbnails, etc.).\\n\\n Looking at the code, there are workers (transcode-worker, export-worker) that process the video. They likely need the video file to be present on the server\\'s disk.\\n\\n Let me check the workers:\\n\\n We have:\\n   src/lib/queue/workers/upload-worker.ts\\n   src/lib/queue/workers/export-worker.ts\\n   src/lib/queue/workers/transcode-worker.ts\\n\\n Let\\'s look at one of them to see how they get the file.\\n\\n But note: the user says \"no storage is neded\" meaning they don\\'t want to store the video on the server at all? Then how would the workers process it?\\n\\n Alternatively, the user might be thinking of using a service like Redis to store the video? But Redis is not for large binary data.\\n\\n Given the confusion, let me ask the user for clarification? However, the instructions say we should not ask for clarification if we can infer. But we are allowed to use the Agent tool to explore.\\n\\n Since we are in a coding session, we can try to infer from the code and then make changes.\\n\\n Steps:\\n\\n 1. We see that the current storage engine has two modes: cloud (R2) and local (disk).\\n 2. The user wants a third mode: when LOCAL_STORE is true, use the system\\'s temporary directory (on the server) for storage, but then automatically clean up after processing? Or perhaps they mean that the file is stored in the temporary directory and then moved to a perm