to not expect an extra \"uploads/\" in the key. But note that the key we are passing to StorageEngine.getUrl is the relativePath we stored (which is `uploads/${filename}`). And then in the storage API route, we are doing getStoragePath(\"uploads\", key) which adds another \"uploads/\".\\n\\nWe should change the storage API route to not add an extra \"uploads/\" if the key already starts with \"uploads/\". Or better, we should change the way we store the filePath in the database.\\n\\nLet me look at the original code before our changes. Originally, the storage engine\\'s getUrl function returned:\\n   isProd ? `/storage/uploads/${key}` : `/uploads/${key}`\\n\\nAnd the key was just the filename (with a timestamp). So the URL would be `/storage/uploads/${filename}` or `/uploads/${filename}`.\\n\\nThen, in the storage API route (if we had one) we would expect the key to be the filename and we would look in the uploads directory.\\n\\nBut note: we removed theold storage API route? Actually, we didn\\'t have one before. We are creating it now.\\n\\nIn the initial upload route (src/app/api/upload/route.ts) we were using getStoragePath(\"uploads\") to get the absolute directory and then storing the file there. And then we set the filePath in the database to `uploads/${filename}` (which is a relative path from the public directory?).\\n\\nThen, when we wanted to get the URL, we would have used the old StorageEngine.getUrl which would return:\\n   in production: `/storage/uploads/${key}` where key is `uploads/${filename}` -> `/storage/uploads/uploads/${filename}`\\n   in development: `/uploads/${key}` -> `/uploads/uploads/${filename}`\\n\\nThat doesn\\'t seem right either.\\n\\nLet me check the original storage.ts (before our changes) to see what the getUrl function was doing.\\n\\nWe don\\'t have the original, but we can infer from the initial upload route and the fact that the user said the upload/download was not working.\\n\\nGiven the time, let\\'s adjust the storage API